title crypto/eip1559_tx.rye — EIP-1559 typed-transaction (type-2) digest in pure Rye, parity-GREEN
prompt take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the crypto/Rye-first road; Rye prioritized, Glow on green Rye; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates
think Read the decision-wave design, the six-season double-seat, the 1024-round itinerary, and REMEMBER. The eip155_tx commit (HEAD) named the next rung outright — "an EIP-1559 typed-transaction (type-2) digest over the same RLP." That is the Lindy-first crux-first move: the digest every modern Ethereum send has signed since the London fork, read for years, self-contained, parity-checkable, authoring no new cryptography and no new serialization.
think The type-2 shape adds exactly one thing over EIP-155: the EIP-2718 envelope. The signing preimage is 0x02 ‖ rlp([chainId, nonce, maxPriorityFeePerGas, maxFeePerGas, gasLimit, to, value, data, accessList]) — nine fields, the accessList itself an RLP list (empty 0xc0 for a plain transfer) — and the signed wire form appends [yParity, r, s] for twelve fields. The recovery bit is a plain yParity (0/1), not the legacy v, because the chain id already lives inside the signed list and needs no folding.
think Parity without a fabricated external constant. EIP-1559 publishes no numeric sighash the way EIP-155 does, and I would not hardcode a vector I could not verify. Instead I bound to the world through a PUBLISHED test key: EIP-155's own example key 0x4646…46, whose address 0x9d8a62f6…855a4f already lives in eip155_tx.rye. Three replayable proofs — (1) deriving that key through our GREEN secp256k1 + eth_address yields exactly that published address; (2) the type-prefixed preimage hashed a second time by Zig's own std.crypto Keccak-256 is byte-for-byte equal to our path; (3) a full round-trip — deterministically signing the sighash with that test key (RFC-6979) then recovering the sender returns exactly the derived address, so a one-bit error in the type prefix, the RLP layout, the hash, the signer, or the recovery would break the match. No custody gate: a public test key, never the maintainer's identity.
think Authored signing_preimage (0x02 then rlp of the nine fields into a bounded max_tx_bytes buffer), signing_sighash (keccak256 of the preimage), and recover_sender (ecr.recover then eth.address_from_pubkey, yParity refused above 1), every function carrying contract asserts. Composes the existing crypto/encoding_rlp.rye symlink shim plus keccak256, secp256k1_ecdsa_sign, secp256k1_ecrecover, eth_address — all already crypto/ files, so no new shim was needed.
obs One mechanical red, caught and fixed: the signed-tx field walk used decode_bytes on every field, but the accessList field is an RLP list (first byte >= 0xc0), so it raised WrongShape. Made the walk shape-aware — decode_list_header for a list byte, decode_bytes otherwise — and the twelve-field frame consumed exactly. GREEN on the next build.
obs Bumped the count guard to 43: registered crypto_eip1559_tx_witness in the suite, updated the stale header comment (was 33/4/37) and both suite prose lines to thirty-four/nine/forty-three, and synced the front doors — crypto/README.md heading and witness count, docs/CRYPTO.md summary, rung-table heading, and witness count. The count-guard bijection prints 43, and the full suite greens all 43 files fresh in dependency order.
obs Signing itself stays the custody gate — this module builds the public preimage and recovers from a signature over a published test key, never holding a real key. A real signature with the maintainer's identity remains gate #3/#4.
